# ⌘S's slide is timed from the first frame it draws, not from the call (SettleClock, as the space settle since
# #613). The slide lasts 85 ms, about ten frames at 120 Hz; the display link's first tick lands a refresh or two
# after ⌘S plus whatever the handler still does, and timed from the call that time was gone before anything drew,
# so the first frame skipped two or three of the ten. Held 80 ms after the call, the first frame still goes one
# frame's way: about 22 % at 120 Hz, 42 % at 60 Hz (Arc's first 60 fps frame: 42–44 %), not most of it.
budget rss=700 stalls50=10
cra://debug?windowSize=1280x800
cra://open?url=${PAGE}?n=slide
expect-log loaded 127\.0\.0\.1 \(HTTP 200\)
wait 1
cra://debug?slideStall=80
expect-log sidebar slide: the first frame went ([0-9]|[1-4][0-9]) % of the way, ([89][0-9]|[1-9][0-9]{2}) ms after the call
# The end readout counts frames drawn (one sample per display-link tick, no 240 Hz timer) and how evenly they came.
expect-log sidebar slide: page moved [0-9]+ pt over [0-9]+ frames \(.*\) at [0-9]+ Hz, [0-9]+ missed, longest [0-9]+ ms
# And back without a stall.
cra://action/toggleSidebar
wait 0.5
# Neither slide's first frame went more than half the way.
reject-log sidebar slide: the first frame went ([5-9][0-9]|100) %
